  "Question": "The roots of \\((x^2-3x+2)(x)(x-4)=0\\) are:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(4\\)",
    "B": "\\(0\\) and \\(4\\)",
    "C": "\\(1\\) and \\(2\\)",
    "D": "\\(0,\\) \\(1,\\) \\(2,\\) and \\(4\\)",
    "E": "\\(1,\\) \\(2,\\) and \\(4\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The equation is already a product equal to zero, so keeping its factors visible makes the roots easy to find. Since \\((-1)+(-2)=-3\\) and \\((-1)(-2)=2\\),\n\\[\nx^2-3x+2=(x-1)(x-2).\n\\]\nThus the original equation is equivalent to\n\\[\nx(x-1)(x-2)(x-4)=0.\n\\]\nA product is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero. The four factors give \\(x=0,1,2,4\\). Each listed value makes a factor zero, and every other value makes all four factors nonzero, so there are no additional roots. The answer is \\(\\boxed{0,1,2,4}\\), choice D.",
